STATEMENT 4: UpdateProductAsync - UPDATE Transaction with Variable Storage
================================================================================
Status: DMS_FAILURE - MANUAL_CONVERSION_APPLIED

DMS Tool Input:
---------------
Schema Name: dbo
SQL Text: BEGIN TRANSACTION; DECLARE @OldPrice DECIMAL(18,2); DECLARE @OldStock INT; SELECT @OldPrice = Price, @OldStock = StockQuantity FROM Products WHERE ProductId = @ProductId; UPDATE Products SET Name = @Name, Description = @Description, Price = @Price, StockQuantity = @StockQuantity, ModifiedDate = GETDATE() WHERE ProductId = @ProductId; INSERT INTO ProductHistory (ProductId, Action, OldPrice, NewPrice, OldStock, NewStock, ActionDate) VALUES (@ProductId, 'UPDATE', @OldPrice, @Price, @OldStock, @StockQuantity, GETDATE()); UPDATE ProductStats SET AveragePrice = (AveragePrice * TotalProducts - @OldPrice + @Price) / TotalProducts, LastUpdated = GETDATE() WHERE StatId = 1; COMMIT

DMS Tool Output:
----------------
Error: Same metadata model creation failure

Manual Conversion Applied:
---------------------------
Changes for PostgreSQL compatibility:
1. Parameters: @ProductId→$1, @Name→$2, @Description→$3, @Price→$4, @StockQuantity→$5
2. Variable assignment: SELECT @Var = Col → SELECT Col INTO v_Var
3. Variable naming: @OldPrice → v_OldPrice, @OldStock → v_OldStock
4. GETDATE() → CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
5. Transaction managed by ADO.NET code

For ADO.NET implementation, broke into sequential commands:
- First command retrieves old values
- Subsequent commands use those values as parameters

Conversion Decision Rationale:
-------------------------------
PostgreSQL uses different syntax for variable assignment in SELECT statements (INTO clause). Transaction management is better handled at the ADO.NET level for consistency with other operations. Multiple statements are executed sequentially within a single transaction controlled by the code.

